<BaseLayout
  title="Safety — Burning Incense Responsibly — LanDeng"
  description="Safety guidance for burning Chinese botanical incense — ventilation, smoke, and responsible use."
>
  <article class="article">
    <h1>Safety</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Incense is a burning material. Used responsibly, it is a pleasant aromatic practice; used
      carelessly, it presents real risks. This page is our transparent safety disclosure.
    </p>

    <h2>Combustion and smoke</h2>
    <p>
      Burning incense produces <strong>smoke and particulate matter</strong>. We do not claim incense
      smoke is harmless — combustion of any plant material produces particles you should not inhale
      in concentration.
    </p>

    <h2>Responsible use</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Burn in a <strong>well-ventilated</strong> space.</li>
      <li>Avoid inhaling concentrated smoke directly.</li>
      <li>Use a proper, heat-safe holder or burner.</li>
      <li>Never leave burning incense unattended.</li>
      <li>Keep away from children, pets, and flammable materials.</li>
      <li>Allow ash to cool before disposing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Natural does not mean safe</h2>
    <p>
      A material being "natural" or "botanical" does <strong>not</strong> mean it is automatically
      safe to inhale or consume. This is an important distinction we hold ourselves to. We do not make
      medical or health claims, and we do not describe incense as a treatment for any condition.
    </p>

    <h2>Traditional use vs medical use</h2>
    <p>
      Traditional and cultural uses of these materials (ritual, meditation, materia medica) are
      <strong>traditional knowledge</strong>, not modern clinical evidence. Do not use incense as a
      substitute for medical care.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
